WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.718
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.092 --> 00:00:05.550
SPEAKER: Welcome
to the quick start

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.740
for Cloud Storage,
Google Cloud's object

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.660
store for unstructured data
such as images, videos,

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.120
and documents.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.650
In this video,
we're going to walk

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.379
through how to perform basic
tasks in Cloud Storage using

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:18.720
the gsutil command line tool.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.500
To start, visit
console.cloud.google.com

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.940
to open the Google
Cloud Console.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.010
This web portal lets
you manage and explore

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.560
your cloud resources
directly from a browser.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.180
To get started
with Cloud Storage,

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.170
you need a Google Cloud project.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:40.070
You can use an existing
project or set up a new one.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.770
Next, activate Cloud
Shell by clicking its icon

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.230
to the right of the search bar.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.270
Cloud Shell is an online
development environment

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:48.980
that comes preloaded
with helpful tools

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.960
for interacting with
your cloud resources.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.970
Once it launches, click
the Maximize button

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.610
to increase the size of
the Cloud Shell terminal.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.220
Now you'll run some commands
using the gsutil command line

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.380
tool, which allows you to
interact with your Cloud

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.600
Storage resources
from command line.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.140
gsutil stands for
Google Storage Utility.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.993
Now create a Cloud
Storage bucket.

00:01:08.993 --> 00:01:11.160
Buckets are the basic
containers that hold your data

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.370
in Cloud Storage.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:16.430
To create a bucket, run
the gsutil mb command

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.430
and provide a global unique
name for your bucket.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:24.200
This demo uses a bucket
named storage-quickstart-777.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.030
But you must choose
your own bucket name.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.220
Next, I'll put an
object into your bucket.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:37.240
First, run the wget command
to download a kitten.png image

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.800
to the Cloud Shell environment.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.400
Then run the gsutil cp
command to copy the image

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.680
from your local Cloud Shell
environment to your Cloud

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:45.490
Storage bucket.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.010
Congratulations,
you've successfully

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.840
outputted an object
to your bucket.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.670
Now download the object
from Cloud Storage

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:57.650
back to your local
Cloud Shell environment

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.830
using the gsutil
cp command again.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:05.890
You'll download the object
as a file called kitten2.png.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:10.610
If you run ls, you can see that
it was downloaded successfully.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.380
Within Cloud
Storage buckets, you

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.230
can create folders to
organize your objects.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.140
With gsutil cp, you
can upload an object

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:21.420
to a specific folder in
a Cloud Storage bucket.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.580
This command creates a
folder called folder1

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.120
within your bucket and uploads
the kitten image there.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:31.170
You can also list the contents
of a bucket or folder using

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.810
the gsutil ls command.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.910
Here, you can list the
contents at the top level

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:37.230
of your bucket.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:42.020
The two items listed are
kitten.png and folder1.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.990
You can also list details for
an individual object using

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:48.750
gsutil ls with the -l flag.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:53.040
Here, you can list details about
kitten.png, such as its size

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.180
and date of creation.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.820
Now let's explore how to manage
Cloud Storage permissions.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:02.000
Run the gsutil iam
ch command, which

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.550
allows you to change permissions
for a given Cloud Storage

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:05.830
bucket.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:09.010
Here, you specify that you want
to give all users permission

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.980
to view objects
stored in the bucket.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:15.640
The command is successful
if no error is returned.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.280
To remove public view
access to these objects,

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:24.090
run the gsutil iam ch
command with the -d flag.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.980
You may also want to
give a specific person

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.930
access to your bucket.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:31.950
This command gives the
gcpstudentdevelopers7@gmail.com

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:35.760
account permission to read and
write object to your bucket.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.230
To remove this permission, run
the same gsutil iam ch command,

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.000
but with the -d flag.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.420
To delete an object, run
this gsutil rm command

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.020
to delete kitten.png from
the Cloud Storage bucket.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.870
And finally, run this command
to delete the Cloud Storage

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:52.800
bucket.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.920
The -r flag is added so that
everything inside of the bucket

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.150
is deleted as well.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:01.880
And that's it.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:03.710
You're now equipped
to perform basic tasks

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:07.870
in Cloud Storage using the
gsutil command line tool.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:09.670
While this video
used Cloud Shell,

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:12.880
you can also use gsutil
from your local computer.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:15.580
Check out the gsutil Getting
Started Guide, linked

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.610
in the description below, to
learn how to install gsutil

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:20.973
on your local computer.

00:04:20.973 --> 00:04:23.140
Make sure to check out the
rest of our Cloud Storage

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:27.978
documentation at
cloud.google.com/storage/docs,

00:04:27.978 --> 00:04:30.520
so you can learn more about how
to store and manage your data

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:31.240
in Google Cloud.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.290
[MUSIC PLAYING]

